SQUARE-PYRAMIDAL CUBOID PACKING
===============================
Faulhaber at p=2 gives the square-pyramidal numbers
  P_2(n)=1^2+...+n^2 = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6.
Hence
  6*P_2(n) = n(n+1)(2n+1) = u(2n+1),
so six n-step square pyramids fill the u x (2n+1) cuboid.
Equivalently, one doubled and four regular pyramids also account for the
same cuboid because 2+4=6 at the level of the volume identity.

This is the p=2 analogue of the p=1 staircase triangle identity
  2*T_n = n(n+1) = u,
so the first two exact anchors are rectangle/cuboid packings with no
Bernoulli correction, while p>=3 acquires the Faulhaber offset alpha+beta/u+...

Sample values:
  n=1: T_n=1, 2*T_n=2=u=2, P_2(n)=1, 6*P_2(n)=6=u*(2n+1)=6
  n=2: T_n=3, 2*T_n=6=u=6, P_2(n)=5, 6*P_2(n)=30=u*(2n+1)=30
  n=3: T_n=6, 2*T_n=12=u=12, P_2(n)=14, 6*P_2(n)=84=u*(2n+1)=84
  n=4: T_n=10, 2*T_n=20=u=20, P_2(n)=30, 6*P_2(n)=180=u*(2n+1)=180
  n=5: T_n=15, 2*T_n=30=u=30, P_2(n)=55, 6*P_2(n)=330=u*(2n+1)=330
  n=6: T_n=21, 2*T_n=42=u=42, P_2(n)=91, 6*P_2(n)=546=u*(2n+1)=546

Also
  S_1(n)=n(n+1)(2n+1)/2 = 3*P_2(n),
so the p=1 common sum is already three square pyramids.

LIVE BALANCE POLYNOMIALS AT n=1
================================
At n=1 the balance polynomial is
  D_p(C,1) = C^p + (C-1)^p - (C+1)^p.
For even p, C=0 is a forced symmetry root, so the live factor is D_p(C,1)/C.
This is the smallest row where higher-p irrationality is visible without
any asymptotic expansion.

Exact low-power cases:
  p=1: C - 2
  p=2: C - 4     after removing the forced C factor

Irreducible live factors for p=3..12 (finite-field certificates):
  p= 3: C^3 - 6*C^2 - 2    irreducible mod 5
  p= 4: C^3 - 8*C^2 - 8    irreducible mod 7
  p= 5: C^5 - 10*C^4 - 20*C^2 - 2    irreducible mod 23
  p= 6: C^5 - 12*C^4 - 40*C^2 - 12    irreducible mod 17
  p= 7: C^7 - 14*C^6 - 70*C^4 - 42*C^2 - 2    irreducible mod 37
  p= 8: C^7 - 16*C^6 - 112*C^4 - 112*C^2 - 16    irreducible mod 13
  p= 9: C^9 - 18*C^8 - 168*C^6 - 252*C^4 - 72*C^2 - 2    irreducible mod 11
  p=10: C^9 - 20*C^8 - 240*C^6 - 504*C^4 - 240*C^2 - 20    irreducible mod 7
  p=11: C^11 - 22*C^10 - 330*C^8 - 924*C^6 - 660*C^4 - 110*C^2 - 2    irreducible mod 19
  p=12: C^11 - 24*C^10 - 440*C^8 - 1584*C^6 - 1584*C^4 - 440*C^2 - 24    irreducible mod 109

Certificate summary through p=20:
  p=3:q=5, p=4:q=7, p=5:q=23, p=6:q=17, p=7:q=37, p=8:q=13, p=9:q=11, p=10:q=7, p=11:q=19, p=12:q=109, p=13:q=43, p=14:q=11, p=15:q=19, p=16:q=13, p=17:q=41, p=18:q=157, p=19:q=197, p=20:q=37

Interpretation:
  The tower cases p=1,2 are the last rows whose live n=1 balance factors
  down to degree 1. From p>=3 onward, the first visible balance polynomial
  is already irreducible in the stored window, so the higher anchors are
  algebraic but not split by the old tower mechanism.

TOURNAMENT ANALYSIS
===================
Assumption challenge:
  Vertices were not taken to be powers p, interval entries, or odd moments
  alone. The chosen quotient uses proof carriers, preserving exactness,
  packing geometry, asymptotic reach, irreducibility signal, support-transfer
  value, and proof reach while discarding decorative row data.

Pairwise observable:
  majority over (exactness, geometry, generality, irreducibility,
  support_transfer, proof_reach), with the listed carrier order as the
  fixed tie Hamiltonian path.

Vertices:
  rectangle_packing: (exact=5, geom=5, gen=1, irr=0, support=1, reach=2)
  square_pyramidal_cuboid: (exact=5, geom=5, gen=2, irr=1, support=2, reach=3)
  odd_moment_reduction: (exact=5, geom=2, gen=5, irr=3, support=4, reach=5)
  alpha_beta_asymptotic: (exact=4, geom=1, gen=4, irr=2, support=3, reach=4)
  n1_live_irreducibility: (exact=4, geom=0, gen=3, irr=5, support=5, reach=4)
  78_90_support_shadow: (exact=4, geom=3, gen=2, irr=1, support=5, reach=3)
  global_bracket_tail: (exact=1, geom=1, gen=5, irr=2, support=3, reach=5)
  hidden_lift_transfer: (exact=2, geom=0, gen=3, irr=4, support=5, reach=3)

Tournament fingerprint:
  ranking leader = odd_moment_reduction
  score_hist = {0: 1, 2: 2, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 2, 7: 1}
  directed_3cycles = 4
  scc_sizes = [6, 1, 1]
  hamiltonian_paths = 17
  edge_flips_vs_exactness_only = 14

Readout:
  The leader is odd_moment_reduction, not the exact p=1/p=2 packings and
  not the 78/90 shadow. The tournament therefore prefers the carrier that
  simultaneously explains all p, produces the asymptotic, and interfaces
  with the irreducibility and hidden-lift lanes.

TAKEAWAYS
=========
1. The midpoint c=a+n is centered at p*u, where u=n(n+1), for every power p.
2. Only odd Faulhaber moments survive in the exact balance equation.
3. The first two Bernoulli/Faulhaber corrections are
     +(p-1)(p-2)/(12p) and
     -(p-1)(p-2)(2p^2-4p-1)/(180 p^3 u).
4. p=1 and p=2 are exactly the no-correction cases; from p>=3 onward the
   same triangular carrier persists but acquires Bernoulli address terms.
5. The first visible higher-p live balance polynomial (n=1) is already
   irreducible in the stored p=3..20 window, so the tower split stops
   exactly where the Bernoulli/Faulhaber corrections begin.
